INT. RIDESHARE CAR - NIGHT

The Corolla smells of pine air freshener and old fries. The dashboard clock says 2:04 and the wipers drag across a windshield that is barely wet, a drizzle that cant decide to become rain. Mo has been driving eleven hours. He keeps the radio low, some call-in show about gardening, because silence in this car has a way of getting loud.

The back door opens. A woman folds herself into the seat behind him with a duffel bag held against her chest like something that might be taken from her. She does not say hello. She is maybe forty, her coat is too light for the season, and there is a smear of dried mascara she hasnt noticed or has stopped caring about.

THE WOMAN
Fourteen Alder Court.

MO
That's what the app says. You're, uh, Rhea?

THE WOMAN
Whatever it says.

He checks the mirror. She is looking out of the window at nothing, at the shuttered laundromat and the one bright gas station, and he recognises the look, he has seen it on fathers outside delivery rooms and on men outside courtrooms. It is the look of someone rehearsing.

He pulls out. The gardening show talks about slugs.

MO
Long night?

THE WOMAN
Don't do that.

MO
Do what?

THE WOMAN
The thing where you're nice so I'll talk. I'm paying for a ride. Not a conversation.

He lets that sit. In the mirror her jaw works. She unzips the duffel an inch, looks inside, zips it again. It is a small movement and he files it away the way he files away everything, the plates that linger behind him, the bars that empty out at the wrong time. Twelve years of this has made him a reluctant expert in other peoples bad nights.

MO
Okay. Radio's yours if you want it.

She doesn't want it. The car rolls under a yellow light that blinks and blinks for no one.

THE WOMAN
Is it far? From here.

MO
Nine minutes. Maybe ten.

THE WOMAN
Can you make it twelve.

He glances back. She holds his eyes in the mirror for the first time and there is something so naked in it that he looks away first.

MO
I can drive slow.

THE WOMAN
Thank you.

He takes the long way along the river road. Black water to the left, the shapes of warehouses to the right. She counts something under her breath, streetlights maybe, or the days. He cannot tell.

MO
My daughter's about your age. Well. Younger. Twenty-six.

THE WOMAN
I said not a conversation.

MO
You did. Sorry.

THE WOMAN
Does she call you?

He doesnt answer right away. His thumb taps the wheel.

MO
Sundays. Mostly.

THE WOMAN
Mostly.

MO
Mostly.

She nods slowly, as if he has confirmed a theory. Then her phone lights up in her lap and she flinches like it burned her. She turns it face down. It lights up again through her fingers, a pale glow around the edges of her hand, and again, and she presses her whole palm over it.

MO (CONT'D)
You can pick up. I don't mind.

THE WOMAN
It's my brother.

MO
Bad news kind of brother?

THE WOMAN
The kind that's right.

That lands somewhere in him. He slows for no reason at an empty crosswalk.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
He said don't come. He said she wouldn't want it. Our mother. That after nine years she's not going to want me standing in her hallway at two in the morning with a bag.

MO
Is that what's in the bag?

THE WOMAN
Her things. What I took. I took a lot.

He nods like he understands, and it surprises him to find that he does, a little, that there is a version of him in a different car on a different night carrying a different bag up a different driveway.

The phone buzzes again. She finally turns it over and reads and her face does something complicated.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
He says the porch light's on.

MO
Is that good?

THE WOMAN
She leaves it on every night. He says she always has. Since I left.

She laughs, one cracked note, and then she is crying, quietly and efficiently, the way people cry who have practiced it alone in cars. He keeps his eyes on the road. He reaches across and pulls a napkin from the glovebox without looking and holds it back between the seats. She takes it.

THE WOMAN (CONT'D)
Rhea's not my name. It's my sisters. I use hers on the app.

MO
Okay.

THE WOMAN
It's Tamsin. I'm Tamsin.

MO
I'm Mo.

TAMSIN
I know. It says on the app. Your picture's older than you.

MO
It's from a better year.

She almost smiles. The river road bends away from the water and the houses start, porch after porch, dark windows and sleeping cars. Alder Court is two turns ahead and they both know it. He takes the first turn very slowly.

TAMSIN
What if I get out and I can't walk to the door.

MO
Then you sit in the car a while. The meter's not running. I'll say it's broke.

TAMSIN
Is it broke?

MO
It's whatever you need it to be.

He turns onto Alder Court. Number fourteen is a narrow house with green shutters and, over the door, a single yellow bulb, burning, with moths turning around it like slow thoughts. He stops the car a little short of it and puts it in park. The engine ticks. The gardening man says goodnight.

She does not move. He does not make her. Her hand finds the door handle and rests there, and for a long time that is all that happens in the car, two people and a yellow light, until upstairs in the house a curtain shifts.

TAMSIN
Oh god. She's up.

MO
Maybe she never went to bed.

Tamsin picks up the duffel. Her hands are shaking so badly the zipper rattles. She opens the door and the cold comes in and the drizzle, finally, begins to be rain.

TAMSIN
Wait here? Just till I'm inside.

MO
I'll wait till the light goes off. Or you tell me to go.

She steps out. She looks at the house. Then she leans back down to the window, rain on her face, and for a second she looks about twenty, a girl in a doorway of her own making.

TAMSIN
Five stars.

MO
Go on.

She goes up the path. The front door opens before she reaches it and a small gray figure stands in the yellow light with one hand over her mouth, and Mo, who has seen a lot of doors, looks away to give them the dignity of it, and turns the radio off.
